why: operator directive 2026-09-07 — "выясни причину зависания и
  исправь". Found by bench-refresh: BUGS.md `own-long-join-deadlock`.
  `AdversarialBenchmark.forkJoin10k_okayOwnLongInside` sat 62 min at
  0.0% CPU on master, the joining thread parked in Platform.scala:87
  and all 42 `Schedulers$Owned$Worker` parked at Platform.scala:542.

what: (1) reproduce it OUTSIDE JMH, in a loop, and establish whether
  it is deterministic or intermittent — a rate, not an adjective;
  (2) find the actual mechanism from a thread dump of the repro, not
  from reading the comment at Platform.scala:542; (3) put the shape
  into src/test/scala-jvm/TestSchedulerLaws.scala, which already runs
  every law against `own.forLongTasks` and already has a "no lost
  wake" law at ONE worker — this is the same law at the shape that
  broke; (4) watch the new test FAIL first; (5) fix Platform.scala;
  (6) BUGS.md -> status: fixed, fixed-in: <sha>, gate: the new law.

predict: a lost wakeup in the park at Platform.scala:542 — the worker
  publishes `parked` and leaves `awake` before its last look at the
  queues, and the submitter's unpark is missed. That is the entry's
  READING of the comment and may well be wrong; step 2 exists to
  disagree with it. Disqualifying evidence: if the repro shows the
  joined fiber's answer never WRITTEN (rather than written-but-not-
  woken), the bug is in DriveTask/joinAsync, not in the worker park.

